{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in modern filmmaking, blending razor-sharp dialogue with a gripping narrative that dissects ambition, betrayal, and the birth of an empire. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film chronicles the rise of facebook through the eyes of its controversial founder, mark zuckerberg, played with icy precision by jesse eisenberg.\n\nthe story unfolds with a sense of inevitability, yet the journey is anything but predictable. it begins with zuckerberg, a harvard undergraduate, coding thefacebook.com in his dorm room after being dumped by his girlfriend. what follows is a whirlwind of lawsuits, power plays, and personal downfalls, all set against the backdrop of tech’s wild west in the early 2000s. the film’s pacing is relentless, mirroring the breakneck speed of the internet’s expansion and the cutthroat nature of silicon valley.\n\njesse eisenberg’s performance is a marvel—capturing zuckerberg’s genius, social awkwardness, and simmering resentment with unsettling accuracy. andrew garfield, as Eduardo savarin, delivers a heartbreaking turn as zuckerberg’s best friend and co-founder, whose betrayal fuels much of the film’s drama. armie hammer is equally compelling as the winklevoss twins, their smug entitlement making them both absurd and oddly sympathetic.\n\nthe soundtrack, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is a haunting blend of electronic and orchestral elements, perfectly complementing the film’s themes of isolation and innovation. the cinematography, led by jeff cronenweth, is sleek and stylish, often framing zuckerberg in cold, clinical shots that emphasize his emotional detachment.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a movie about facebook—it’s a meditation on the cost of success, the fragility of friendship, and the blurred lines between genius and ruthlessness. sorkin’s dialogue 